n a function means a normal index on `users.email` can't be used. The database has to scan every row in `users` and apply `lower()` to each one. Pick one of these fixes:\n   - Create an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. This works in Postgres, and MySQL 8.0.13+ supports functional indexes too. Keep the query as it is.\n   - Use a case-insensitive type or collation, such as `citext` in Postgres. You could also store emails already lowercased and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` directly.\n\n2. **`SELECT *` returns more than you need.** It pulls every column from both tables, including duplicate names like `id` and `created_at`. That means more I/O and network traffic, and it stops the planner from using index-only scans. It also makes the result fragile when columns change. List only the columns you need.\n\n3. **`orders.user_id` needs an index.** I can't see the schema, so I'm assuming it isn't indexed. Without `CREATE INDEX ON orders (user_id)`, each matching user triggers a scan of `orders`, or the planner picks a costly hash join over the whole table. Postgres doesn't create indexes on foreign keys automatically.\n\n4. **Uniqueness of email affects the result size.** If `users.email` is unique, a unique index (on `lower(email)` if you go with fix 1) gives a single-row lookup. Then the join cost depends on how many orders that user has. If that number can be large, add a `LIMIT` or pagination, or order by an indexed column.\n\nThe expression index in item 1 and the `orders(user_id)` index in item 3 should give nearly all of the improvement. I haven't run this against a database, so check with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}